The '''Baiduist revolutionary army''' ([[Zong language|Zong]]: 海渡革命軍, ''Hái-tō͘ Kek-bēng-kun'', "Ocean Crossing Revolutionary Army") was the armed force raised by the [[Ocean Crossing Society]] in 1648 and fought in the [[Sinchew War]] of 1648 to 1656. It was formed from the society's branches among the Baiduist settlements of the interior, and its object was the overthrow of the [[Sinchew Colony|colonial administration]] at [[Tamsui]].

It was the first force raised by the Baiduist population of the colony as a body, and after its defeat in the colony's territory it withdrew north-east of Tamsui and became the armed force of [[Free Sinchew]].

== Origin ==

{{Main|Ocean Crossing Society}}

The army was not raised as an army. The Ocean Crossing Society (海渡會, ''Hái-tō͘-hōe'') had been founded in the 1620s among the Baiduist settlements of the interior as a religious and mutual-aid association, and it maintained branches, officers and a membership organised for relief, arbitration and burial.

The society acquired arms in the 1640s, and the concentration of the Baiduist population along the margins of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]] produced the conditions in which a body already organised for mutual aid could be turned to military purposes. It took the settlements of the lake shore in the spring of 1648, and the force it raised from them was the revolutionary army.

== Command ==

The army was commanded by Ng Bú-seng, a Baiduist who had served as a garrison commander in the [[Third Mahik War]]. He was the only officer of the force with experience of command in a campaign, and the society's branches supplied its subordinate officers.

The army was financed by Baiduist merchant houses holding property in the colony. Its arms came from outside the Zong colonial sphere: during the war the colonial authorities of the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]] supplied firearms and materiel through smuggling networks across the isthmus, and the supply included cannon and siege equipment as well as small arms. The traffic was conducted covertly and was not known to the colony or to the Zong court until the settlement negotiations of 1656.

== Strength and composition ==

The army numbered about 25,000 at its height. It was a popular force rather than a professional one: it marched with its families and its livestock, it had no supply train, and it foraged as it advanced. Its losses over the war are estimated at about 14,700 killed.

Its composition was the Baiduist population of the colony, drawn principally from the settlements of the interior and the lake shore. It had no cavalry and no naval arm, and its artillery was what it received from the isthmus.

== Righteous March ==

{{Main|Righteous March}}

The army left its base at [[Takau]] on the lake on 13 April 1648 and marched on Tamsui, which it reached on 18 May. The distance is about 218 km and the column covered it at rather more than six kilometres a day, a rate consistent with a popular movement marching with its families and foraging.

It was defeated at Tamsui. The First and Second Imperial Fleets of Zong were lying in the bay, and with the colonial garrison they held the city and drove the army back into the highlands on 11 June.

== Course of the war ==

After the defence of Tamsui the army withdrew from the lake country and operated in the interior, where it was pursued rather than destroyed. Its operations after 1648 were directed at settlements near it rather than at the cities, and it was reduced in the colony's own territory during 1655.

The Baiduist population of the colony was divided by the war, and numbers of Baiduists turned against the army in these years to avoid being treated as its supporters.

== Aftermath ==

In the last years of the war the army was in retreat and foraging, and it left the settled country for land that neither [[Hachinawa]] nor the colony had claimed. It withdrew north-east of Tamsui and established [[Free Sinchew]] there, a territory of about 19,174 km², and became that state's armed force.

Free Sinchew was held until 1715, when it was conquered by the Sinchew Colony in the [[First War of the Isthmus]].

